== Chapter IX

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The deceit of sin in drawing the mind away from properly attending to special duties of obedience, shown in meditation and prayer.]
]

#smallcaps[We] have shown how sin by its #emph[deceit] tries to draw the mind away from attending to that holy frame in walking with God in which the soul ought to be preserved. Let us now go on to show how it does the same work with regard to those #emph[special duties] by which its designs, workings, and prevailing power may in a special way be headed off and prevented. Sin indeed keeps up an enmity against all duties of obedience, or rather against God in them. “When I want to do good,” says the apostle, “evil is present with me.” Whenever I want to do good, or whatever good I want to do, good that is spiritual, good with regard to God, sin is present with me to hinder me from it and to oppose me in it. On the other side, all duties of obedience lie directly against the actings of the law of sin. For as the #emph[flesh] in all its actings #emph[lusts] against the Spirit, so the #emph[Spirit] in all its actings #emph[lusts] against the flesh. And so every duty performed in the strength and grace of the Spirit is contrary to the law of sin. Rom 8:13: “if by the Spirit you#super[+] put to death the deeds of the flesh.” The actings of the Spirit of grace in duties do this work. These two are contrary. But there are #emph[some duties] which, in their own nature and by God’s appointment, have a special influence on weakening and subduing the whole law of sin in its very principles and its chief strongholds. These are the duties the mind of a #emph[believer] ought to attend to #emph[principally] in his whole course. And these are the duties sin in its deceit tries #emph[principally] to draw the #emph[mind] away from. In diseases of the body, they say, some remedies have a specific quality against particular disorders. So in this disease of the soul there are some duties that have a #emph[special power] against this sinful disorder. I will not dwell on many of them, but will give only two as instances, which seem to me to be of this nature: that is, by God’s design they have a special tendency toward the ruin of the #emph[law of sin.] Then we will show the ways, methods, and means the #emph[law of sin] uses to divert the mind from properly attending to them. Now these duties are, first, #emph[prayer,] especially private prayer, and secondly, #emph[meditation.] I put them together because they agree closely in their general nature and end, and differ only in the manner of performing them. For by #emph[meditation] I mean meditating on how the #emph[word] and our own #emph[hearts] stand related and suited to each other, with this end, that our hearts may be brought into a more exact conformity to the word. It is our pondering the truth as it is in #emph[Jesus,] to find its image and likeness in our own hearts. So it has the same intent as prayer, which is to bring our souls into a frame that answers in all things to the mind and will of God. They are like the blood and the spirits in the veins, which have the same life, motion, and use. But people are generally at a great loss in this duty of #emph[meditation.] So, having declared it to be of such great efficacy for controlling the actings of the #emph[law of sin,] I will briefly give in passing two or three rules to direct #emph[believers] to the right performance of this great duty. They are these.

First, #emph[meditate on God with God.] That is, when we would take up thoughts and #emph[meditations] of God, of his excellencies, his attributes, his glory, his majesty, his love, his goodness, let it be done by way of #emph[speaking to God,] in a deep humbling and abasement of our souls before him. This will fix the mind, and lead it on from one thing to another to give glory to God in a proper way. It will affect the soul until it is brought into that holy wonder at God and delight in him which is acceptable to him. My meaning is that it should be done by way of #emph[prayer] and #emph[praise,] speaking to God.

Secondly, #emph[meditate on the word in the word.] That is, in reading it, consider the sense of the particular passages we dwell on, looking to God for help, guidance, and direction in discovering his mind and will in them. Then labor to have our hearts affected by it.

Thirdly, whatever we fall short of in evenness and constancy of thought in these things, let it be made up in frequency. Some are discouraged because their minds do not steadily supply them with thoughts to carry on their #emph[meditations,] through the weakness or imperfection of their powers of invention. Let this be made up by #emph[frequent] returns of the mind to the subject set out for meditation; in this way new senses of it will keep being supplied. But this is in passing.

These duties, I say, among others, make a #emph[special opposition] to the very being and life of indwelling sin; or rather #emph[faith] in them does. (We have chosen them only as an instance, not excluding some other duties from the same place, office, and usefulness.) They are continually designing its utter ruin. So, in pursuing our present purpose with this #emph[instance,] I will do these two things.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, show the suitableness and #emph[usefulness] of this duty, or these duties (as I will treat them together), for ruining sin.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, show the #emph[means] by which the deceitfulness of sin tries to #emph[draw the mind away] from properly attending to them.]]

=== The Usefulness of Prayer against Sin

For the first, observe:

First, it is the proper work of the soul in this duty to consider all the #emph[secret workings] and actings of sin: what advantages it has gained, what temptations it is joined with, what harm it has already done, and what further harm it is ready to do. That is why #emph[David] gives this title to one of his prayers, Ps 102: “A prayer of one who is afflicted, when he grows faint and pours out his lament before the #smallcaps[Lord].” I speak of the prayer that goes with a proper consideration of all the needs, straits, and emergencies of the soul. Without this, prayer is not prayer. That is, whatever show or appearance of that duty it has, it is of no use either to the glory of God or to the good of men’s souls. It is a cloud without water, driven by the wind of men’s breath. And no more ready and effectual poison for souls was ever found than binding them to a constant form and use of I know not what words in their prayers and supplications, words they themselves do not understand. Bind men in this way in their trades or their business in this world, and they will quickly find the effect of it. By this means they are made unable to consider properly what is good for them at present or what is evil to them. And without that, what use can prayer serve, except to mock God and delude men’s own souls? But in the kind of prayer we are speaking of, the Spirit of God comes in to give us his help, and in this very matter of #emph[finding out] and uncovering the most secret actings and workings of the law of sin. Rom 8:26: “We do not know what we ought to pray for … the Spirit helps us in our weakness.” He shows us our needs, and where we chiefly need help and relief. And we find by daily experience that in prayer believers are led into such discoveries and convictions of the secret, deceitful work of sin in their hearts as no considerations could ever have led them into. So it was with #emph[David] in Ps 51. He set out to confess his actual sin, and in his prayer his wound was searched by the skillful hand of the Spirit of God. Then the root of all his failures was discovered to him in his #emph[original corruption] (v. 5). In this duty the Spirit is like the “lamp of the #smallcaps[Lord]” to the soul, enabling it to search all its inmost parts. He gives a holy, spiritual light to the mind, enabling it to search the deep and dark recesses of the heart and to find out the subtle and deceitful schemes, fictions, and imaginations of the law of sin there. Whatever notion there is of sin, whatever power and prevailing force in it, it is laid hold of, seized, brought into the presence of God, judged, condemned, and mourned over. And what could possibly be more effectual for its ruin and destruction? For together with its discovery, the soul applies itself to all the relief provided in Jesus Christ against it, all the ways and means by which it may be ruined. That is why it is the duty of the mind “to be watchful in prayer” (1 Pet 4:7): to attend diligently to the state of our souls, and to deal fervently and effectually with God about it. The same may also be said of #emph[meditation,] wisely managed to its proper end.

Secondly, in this duty a deep, full sense of the #emph[vileness of sin] is worked on the heart, with a constantly renewed detestation of it; and if anything tends to its ruin, this undoubtedly does. This is one design of prayer, one end of the soul in it: to draw sin out, to set it in order, and to present it to itself in its vileness, abomination, and aggravating circumstances, so that it may be loathed, abhorred, and cast away as a filthy thing (as in Isa 30:22). He who pleads with God for the remission of sin also pleads with his own heart for its detestation (Hos 14:3). In this also sin is #emph[judged] in the name of God. For in its confession the soul puts its name to God’s detestation of sin, and to the sentence of his law against it. There is indeed a #emph[round] of these duties that convinced persons give themselves up to, as a mere cover for their lusts. They cannot sin quietly unless they perform their duty constantly. But the prayer we speak of is a thing of another nature. It will allow no compromise with sin, much less will it serve the ends of sin’s deceit, as that other formal prayer does. It will not be #emph[bribed] into a secret compliance with any of the enemies of God or of the soul, no, not for a moment. That is why often in this duty the heart is raised to the most sincere and effectual sense of sin, and detestation of it, that the soul ever attains in its whole course of obedience. And this plainly tends also to the #emph[weakening] and ruin of the law of sin.

Thirdly, this is the way God has appointed and blessed for obtaining strength and power against sin. Jas 1:5: “If any of you lacks …, he should ask God.” Prayer is the way of obtaining from God by Christ a supply of all our needs, and help against all opposition, especially the opposition made against us by sin. This, I suppose, need not be dwelt on; it is clear to every believer, both in its notion and in practice. It is the means by which we #emph[call,] and on which the Lord Jesus comes in to our #emph[aid] with suitable help in a time of need (Heb 2:17).

Fourthly, faith in prayer #emph[counters] all the workings of the deceit of sin. That is because in prayer the soul constantly binds itself to God to oppose all sin whatever. Ps 119:106: “I have sworn and confirmed that I will keep Your righteous judgments.” This is the language of every gracious soul when it comes before God. Its inmost parts bind themselves to God, to cling to him in all things and to oppose sin in all things. He who cannot do this cannot pray. To pray in any other frame is to flatter God with our lips, which he abhors. And this helps a believer exceedingly in #emph[pursuing sin] to its ruin. For:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, if there is any secret lust lurking in the heart, he will find it either rising up against this engagement or using its #emph[tricks] to keep itself safe from it. By this the lust is discovered, and the heart’s conviction of its evil is furthered and strengthened. Sin makes the most certain #emph[discovery] of itself, and never more plainly than when it is most severely pursued. Lusts in men are compared to harmful and #emph[noxious beasts,] or men themselves are, because of their lusts (Isa 11:4, 5). Now such beasts keep to their dens and hiding places, and never show themselves, at least never so much in their own nature and rage, as when they are most #emph[earnestly pursued.] So it is with sin and corruption in the heart.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, if any sin is #emph[prevailing] in the soul, it will weaken the soul and draw it back from the #emph[universality] of this engagement to God. It will breed a #emph[shrinking back] from it, a slightness in it. Now when this is noticed, it will greatly awaken a gracious soul and stir it up to look about it. A #emph[spontaneous lassitude,] that is, a causeless weariness and indisposition of the body, is looked on as the sign of an approaching fever or some dangerous disorder, and it stirs men up to take timely and vigorous measures, so that they are not seized by it. So it is in this case. When the soul of a believer finds in itself an #emph[indisposition] to make fervent, sincere engagements of universal holiness to God, it knows there is some prevailing disorder in it. It finds where the disorder lies and sets itself against it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, while the soul can constantly bind itself to God in this way, it is certain that sin can rise to no #emph[ruinous prevailing power.] Indeed, it is a #emph[conquest] over sin, a most considerable conquest, when the soul comes through such an engagement fully and clearly, without any secret reserve, with eagerness and resolution, as in Ps 18:23. And upon such a success it may triumph in the grace of God, and have good hope through faith that it will have a final conquest, and that what it so resolves will be done; that it has decreed a thing, and it will be established. And this tends to the defeat, indeed to the ruin, of the law of sin.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, unless the heart is deceived by cursed #emph[hypocrisy,] this engagement to God will greatly move it to a special diligence and watchfulness against all sin. There is no greater evidence of hypocrisy than to have a heart like the #emph[adulterous woman] (Prov 7:14), to say, “Today I have paid my vows; now I may give myself to my sin.” Or to be #emph[careless] about sin, as being #emph[satisfied] that it has prayed against it. It is otherwise in a gracious soul. The sense and conscience of engagements against sin made to God make it watchful in every way against all sin’s motions and workings. On these and various other accounts faith in this duty puts itself forth in a special way, to weaken the power and stop the progress of the law of sin.]]

=== How Sin Draws the Mind Off from Prayer

If, then, the mind is #emph[diligent] in its watch and charge to preserve the soul from the efficacy of sin, it will carefully attend to this duty and to its proper performance, since it is of such singular advantage to the mind’s end and purpose. So here sin puts forth its deceit in its own defense. It labors to divert and draw the mind away from attending to this duty and others like it. Among others, there are #emph[three engines,] three ways and means, by which it tries to accomplish its design.

First, it takes advantage of the weariness this duty brings to the flesh. As has been shown, there is an #emph[aversion] in the law of sin to all immediate communion with God. Now this duty is such communion. Nothing goes with it by which the carnal part of the soul may be gratified or satisfied, as there may be something of that kind in most #emph[public duties,] in most things a man can do beyond pure acts of faith and love. So since no relief or advantage comes by it except what is purely spiritual, it becomes wearisome and burdensome to flesh and blood. It is like traveling alone without companion or diversion, which makes the way seem long, but brings the traveler to his journey’s end with most speed. So our Savior declares. When he expected that his #emph[disciples,] in keeping with their duty and their present distress, would have been engaged in this work, he found them “fast asleep” (Matt 26:41). “The spirit,” he says, “is willing, but the flesh is weak.” And out of that weakness grew their indisposition toward their duty and their weariness of it. So God complains of his people in Isa 43:22: “You have grown weary of Me.” And at length it may come to the height mentioned in Mal 1:13: “You#super[+] also say, ‘Oh, what a weariness!’ And you#super[+] sniff at it, says the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts.” The #emph[Jews] suppose it was the language of men when they brought their offerings or sacrifices on their shoulders. They pretended these #emph[wearied] them, and they panted and puffed like men ready to faint under them, when they brought only the torn, the lame, and the sick. But this duty is often like that to the flesh. And the deceitfulness of sin makes use of this to draw the heart away by imperceptible degrees from a constant attending to it. It puts in a plea for the relief of the weak and weary flesh. In this matter there is a collusion between #emph[spiritual flesh] and #emph[natural flesh.] They help each other, and an aversion to this duty is the effect of their collusion. So it was with the #emph[bride] in Song 5:2, 3. She was asleep, drowsing in her #emph[spiritual condition,] and she pleads her #emph[natural unfitness] to rouse herself from that state. The mind must be diligently watchful to prevent suggestions from this quarter. It must dwell constantly on the considerations that show that attending to this duty is indispensable. It must stir up the principle of grace in the heart to keep its rule and sovereignty and not to be trifled with by foolish pretenses. If it does not, it will be #emph[drawn] away, which is the effect sin aims at.

Secondly, the deceitfulness of sin makes use of corrupt reasonings taken from the pressing and urgent #emph[business of life.] “If we attended strictly to all duties of this kind,” it says in the heart, “we would neglect our chief business and be #emph[useless] to ourselves and others in the world.” And on this general ground, particular #emph[pieces of business] push particular #emph[duties] out of their proper place and time. Men have no leisure to glorify God and save their own souls. It is certain that God gives us time enough for all that he requires of us of any kind in this world. No duties need to #emph[jostle] one another, I mean constantly. Special occasions must be decided according to special circumstances. But if in anything we take more on ourselves than we have time to perform well without robbing God, and our own souls, of what is due to them, God does not call us to it, and he does not bless us in it. It would be more tolerable for our duties of holiness and regard to God to encroach on the duties of our callings and employments in this world than the other way around. And yet God does not require even this of us in an ordinary way or course. How little, then, will he bear with what is plainly so much worse on every account whatever! But through the deceitfulness of sin, this is how the souls of men are beguiled. By several degrees they are at length driven from their duty.

Thirdly, it works on the mind to draw it away from attending to this duty by offering a #emph[compensation] to be made in and by other duties, as #emph[Saul] thought to make up for his disobedience by sacrifice. “May not the same duty performed in public, or in the family, be enough?” And if the soul is so foolish as not to answer, “Those things ought to be done, and this not left undone,” it may be ensnared and deceived. For besides the command to it, that is, that we should personally be watchful in prayer, there are, as has been shown, various advantages against the deceit and efficacy of sin in this duty when it is performed in this way, which the more public attending to it does not have. Sin strives to rob the soul of these by this exchange, which it offers the soul by its corrupt reasonings.

Fourthly, I may add here what has a place in all the workings of sin by #emph[deceit:] its feeding the soul with #emph[promises] and purposes of attending more diligently to this duty when occasions allow. By this means it brings the soul to say to its convictions of duty what #emph[Felix] said to #emph[Paul]: “You may go for now. When I find the time, I will call for you.” And by this means the present season and time, which alone is ours, is often lost beyond recovery.

These are some of the ways and means by which the #emph[deceit of sin] tries to #emph[draw the mind away] from properly attending to this duty, which is so specially suited to prevent sin’s progress and prevailing power, and which aims so directly and immediately at its ruin. I might give instances also from other duties with the same tendency. But this may be enough to uncover the nature of this part of the deceit of sin. And this is the first way by which it makes way for further entangling the #emph[affections] and for the conception of sin. When sin has worked this effect on anyone, he is said to be #emph[drawn away,] to be diverted from what he ought constantly to attend to in his mind as he walks before the Lord.

And this will teach us to see and discern where the beginning of our declines and failings in the ways of God lies, whether in our general course or in our attending to special duties. This is of great importance and concern to us. When the beginnings and occasions of a #emph[sickness] or disorder of the body are known, it is a great advantage in directing toward its cure. To call #emph[Zion] back to himself, God shows her where the #emph[beginning] of her #emph[sin] was (Mic 1:13). Now this is for the most part the #emph[beginning of sin] to us: the drawing of the mind away from properly attending in all things to carrying out its duty. The chief care and charge of the soul lies on the mind. If the mind fails in its duty, the whole is betrayed, either in its general frame or in particular failures. The failing of the mind is like the failing of the #emph[watchman] in #emph[Ezekiel]\; the whole is lost by his neglect. So in the #emph[self-scrutiny] and search we are called to, this is what we are to inquire after most diligently. God does not look at what duties we perform by their number and count, or merely by their nature, but at whether we do them with the attention of mind and spirit that he requires. Many men perform duties in a #emph[routine] or course, and do not so much as think of them, as it were. Their minds are filled with other things; duty only takes up so much of their time. This is nothing but an attempt to mock God and deceive their own souls. If you#super[+] would take the true measure of yourselves, then, consider how it is with you#super[+] as to the duty of your minds that we have inquired into. Consider whether you#super[+] have not been #emph[diverted and drawn away] by some of the deceits mentioned. If there are any declines in you#super[+] of any kind, you#super[+] will find that this is where they began. In one way or another your #emph[minds] have been made heedless, careless, slothful, and unsettled, beguiled and drawn away from their duty. Consider the charge in Prov 4:23, 25, 26, 27. May not such a soul say: “If I had attended more diligently, if I had considered more wisely the vile nature of sin, if I had not allowed my mind to be possessed with vain hopes and foolish imaginations by a cursed abuse of gospel grace, if I had not let it be filled with the things of the world and become careless in attending to special duties, I would not be so sick, weak, unprofitable, wounded, declined, and defiled as I am this day. My careless, deceived mind has been the #emph[beginning of sin] and transgression to my soul”? And this discovery will direct the soul to a suitable way of healing and recovery. That will never be brought about by multiplying particular duties, but by restoring the mind (Ps 23:3).

And from this it also appears that the great means of preserving our souls according to the mind and will of God, both in their general frame and in particular duties, is to seek after a sound and steadfast mind. It is a signal grace to have the Spirit of power and love and of a sound mind (2 Tim 1:7): a stable, solid, resolved mind in the things of God, not easily moved, diverted, or changed, not drawn aside; a mind not apt to listen to corrupt reasonings, vain suggestions, or pretenses that would draw it away from its duty. This is what the apostle urges believers to in 1 Cor 15:58: “Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast and immovable. Always excel in the work of the Lord.” The #emph[steadfastness] of our minds, abiding in their duty, is the cause of all our #emph[immovability] and fruitfulness in obedience. And so #emph[Peter] tells us that those who are by any means led away, or “enticed, fall from their own steadfastness” (2 Pet 3:17). And the great charge laid against backsliders is that they are “not steadfast” (Ps 78:37): “their spirit was not steadfast.” For if the soul is safe unless the mind is drawn away from its duty, then the soundness and steadfastness of the mind is its great preservative. There are three parts to this steadfastness of the mind. First, a full purpose of clinging to God in all things. Secondly, a daily renewing and quickening of the heart to carry out this purpose. Thirdly, resolutions against all trifling or bargaining about negligence in carrying it out. But these are not to be spoken of here.
